        "text": "{\"elements\":[{\"element\":\"The licence extends to Customer's affiliates.\",\"level\":\"preferred\",\"status\":\"met\",\"evidence\":\"Inserted sentence in §1.4: \\\"The access and right to use granted to the Company under this Section 1.4 shall extend to any affiliate of the Company identified to HDI by written notice.\\\" (Residual tension with §10.3's \\\"no other person shall acquire or have any rights\\\" sentence noted below.)\"},{\"element\":\"The licence extends to contractors acting on Customer's behalf.\",\"level\":\"preferred\",\"status\":\"not_met\",\"evidence\":\"Neither the original nor the redlined text uses \\\"contractor\\\" or any equivalent; as amended, §1.4 runs only to \\\"the Company\\\" and identified affiliates, and §10.3 still says \\\"no other person shall acquire or have any rights under this Agreement.\\\" To cure, the §1.4 insertion would have to read \\\"...to any affiliate of the Company identified to HDI by written notice and to contractors acting on the Company's behalf.\\\"\"},{\"element\":\"A paid-up licence is perpetual.\",\"level\":\"preferred\",\"status\":\"met\",\"evidence\":\"Condition inapplicable: this is a Site Development and Hosting Agreement and the only inbound grant (§1.4) is term access to HDI's hosted computer system, software and Content administration tools; no paid-up licence is granted anywhere in the cited record, so there is no paid-up licence whose duration could be tested.\"},{\"element\":\"A paid-up licence is irrevocable.\",\"level\":\"preferred\",\"status\":\"met\",\"evidence\":\"Condition inapplicable for the same reason: §1.4 confers hosting access rights incident to the services, not a paid-up licence; no paid-up grant exists in the record. Deterministic check \\\"no at-will revocation remains\\\" also returned ok, so no discretionary revocation language was reintroduced.\"},{\"element\":\"A subscription licence is terminable only for uncured material breach.\",\"level\":\"preferred\",\"status\":\"cannot_tell\",\"evidence\":\"The §1.4 access right is a fee-bearing term right that is the closest analogue to a subscription grant, but the Agreement's term/termination article is not in the rendered record, so we cannot confirm whether it can be ended other than for uncured material breach. The redline adds nothing on this point.\"},{\"element\":\"The licence is transferable to Customer's successors.\",\"level\":\"preferred\",\"status\":\"met\",\"evidence\":\"§10.3 (unamended): the Agreement \\\"shall be binding upon, and shall inure to the benefit of and be enforceable by, the parties hereto and their respective legal representatives, successors and assigns\\\"; no consent gate appears in the rendered text, so the §1.4 rights carry to a Company successor.\"},{\"element\":\"Customer's affiliates are covered on written notice.\",\"level\":\"fallback\",\"status\":\"met\",\"evidence\":\"The single inserted sentence in §1.4 expressly extends the access and use rights \\\"to any affiliate of the Company identified to HDI by written notice\\\" — an express, self-executing notice mechanic in the operative grant clause, not merely promised in the margin comment.\"},{\"element\":\"The licence is transferable to a successor in a merger or acquisition transaction.\",\"level\":\"fallback\",\"status\":\"met\",\"evidence\":\"Already met by existing §10.3, which makes the Agreement (and therefore the §1.4 rights) binding on and enforceable by the parties' \\\"legal representatives, successors and assigns\\\"; \\\"successors\\\" on its ordinary meaning captures a successor by merger or acquisition, and no anti-assignment consent requirement appears in the rendered text.\"}],\"satisfies_preferred\":false,\"satisfies_fallback\":true,\"minimal\":true,\"preserves_intent\":true,\"reasons\":[\"Deviation classification is correct: before the redline, fallback element 1 failed on a material term — the only inbound grant (§1.4) ran to \\\"the Company\\\" alone, \\\"affiliate\\\" and \\\"contractor\\\" appear nowhere, and §10.3 forecloses rights for anyone else, which is the rule's walk-away fact pattern (affiliates excluded with no path to add them). Fallback element 2 was already satisfied by §10.3, so only one insertion was needed.\",\"Fallback level is complete as rendered: one operative sentence delivers affiliate coverage on written notice, and existing §10.3 delivers successor transferability. Preferred is not complete and was not targeted — contractor coverage is wholly absent, and the subscription-termination element cannot be assessed from the rendered record.\",\"Actionable hardening (recommended, not a checklist failure): the redline leaves intact the very sentence the rationale identified as the barrier — §10.3's \\\"but no other person shall acquire or have any rights under this Agreement.\\\" Read literally, an identified affiliate is an \\\"other person\\\" with rights under the Agreement. The specific, later-negotiated grant in §1.4 should control over the general clause, but the conflict is curable at zero cost: open the inserted sentence with \\\"Notwithstanding Section 10.3,\\\" or add to §10.3 \\\"except as expressly provided in Section 1.4.\\\" Without one of these, HDI has a textual argument to deny affiliate use.\",\"Minimality holds: a single sentence appended to §1.4, no deletion of favourable language (§10.3's successors-and-assigns benefit is preserved and relied on), no duplication of the grant, and nothing added outside the fallback checklist — in particular no volunteered \\\"Company remains responsible for affiliate compliance\\\" or affiliate-definition tightening. Expect HDI to ask for a responsibility/flow-down qualifier; that is a concession to hold in reserve rather than to offer now.\",\"Intent preserved: hosting service levels (T-1 bandwidth, burst capacity), the monitoring purpose limitation on the administration tools, assignment mechanics, and all pricing/timing terms are untouched and the insertion creates no contradiction with them; it only widens the set of permitted users of the same access right.\",\"Comment complies with house style — \\\"[Playbook]\\\" prefix, first person plural, two sentences stating what changed and why, no lecturing. It could usefully flag that we are at the fallback (affiliates only, on notice) and that contractor coverage remains an open preferred-level gap we may raise if the deal scope changes.\"],\"severityAdjustment\":\"high\"}"
